WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.218
One small test was useful.

2
00:00:02.218 --> 00:00:05.767
A second team asks to run it too.

3
00:00:05.767 --> 00:00:09.316
That sounds like progress, and it may be.

4
00:00:09.316 --> 00:00:12.865
It also changes the shape of the work.

5
00:00:12.865 --> 00:00:20.851
The first team knew the people, the context, and the reason for every field in its learning plan.

6
00:00:20.851 --> 00:00:22.507
The second team receives a template and a sentence about success.

7
00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:32.046
Chapter Twenty-Four asks what changes at scale: experiments can collide, metrics can mean different things, and a tool can spread faster than a review practice.

8
00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:38.605
Treat the original test from our fictional membership service as a candidate for reuse, not an approved model.

9
00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:39.618
What was the job?

10
00:00:39.618 --> 00:00:41.586
Which participants were in scope?

11
00:00:41.586 --> 00:00:43.136
What did the team observe?

12
00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:44.925
Which result is still pending?

13
00:00:44.925 --> 00:00:51.066
If the new team serves people with a different access need, the first team's conclusion may not travel.

14
00:00:51.066 --> 00:00:53.868
Put the source and exclusion beside the result.

15
00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:56.432
Scaling begins with a transfer of judgment.

16
00:00:56.432 --> 00:01:03.348
The useful question is whether another team can reproduce the decision boundary, not whether it can copy the screen.

17
00:01:03.348 --> 00:01:12.411
Start the scale-readiness checklist with the parts that must travel: definition, owner, evidence, stop condition, and a place to record the next result.

18
00:01:12.411 --> 00:01:15.392
At this point, speed can become a source of error.

19
00:01:15.392 --> 00:01:23.620
The first team may know that an apparent improvement came from a narrow group, or that a support burden rose after the measurement window.

20
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.647
A copied summary loses both facts.

21
00:01:25.647 --> 00:01:29.642
Ask the original owner to mark what another team must verify again.

22
00:01:29.642 --> 00:01:36.558
Make that conversation part of the handoff, then record the answer so the next team does not rely on hallway memory.

23
00:01:36.558 --> 00:01:42.878
The book proposes shared infrastructure: an experiment template, a hypothesis backlog, and a decision log.

24
00:01:42.878 --> 00:01:44.965
These are not three new committees.

25
00:01:44.965 --> 00:01:49.555
They are ways to keep a test understandable after the original team moves on.

26
00:01:49.555 --> 00:01:50.986
Write a one-page record.

27
00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:57.247
It names the customer job, hypothesis, eligibility rule, measure, decision threshold, date, and reviewer.

28
00:01:57.247 --> 00:02:00.526
Add links to the source ledger and any observed result.

29
00:02:00.526 --> 00:02:03.924
Give the next team permission to say its context differs.

30
00:02:03.924 --> 00:02:06.786
A common format makes differences easier to see.

31
00:02:06.786 --> 00:02:11.795
Chapter Twenty-Four also warns that a central learning team can become a bottleneck.

32
00:02:11.795 --> 00:02:19.188
Assign a local owner who can run the test within an agreed boundary, and a cross-team reviewer for conflicts or shared risk.

33
00:02:19.188 --> 00:02:21.692
The governance should fit the consequence.

34
00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:24.077
A copy change might need a quick review.

35
00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:29.562
A change to pricing, access, privacy, or billing needs the people who own those obligations.

36
00:02:29.562 --> 00:02:41.069
This is the practical meaning of light governance: enough common language to coordinate, enough local authority to keep learning, and a clear escalation path when the cost of being wrong rises.

37
00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:46.018
The record should help work move, not make people prove that they filled every box.

38
00:02:46.018 --> 00:02:48.641
Use the template as a doorway into judgment.

39
00:02:48.641 --> 00:02:54.305
A reviewer should be able to spot a missing source or conflicting test without reading a novel.

40
00:02:54.305 --> 00:02:59.016
Show who has authority to pause work when an access or privacy problem appears.

41
00:02:59.016 --> 00:03:01.520
Write the escalation in ordinary language.

42
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.661
If nobody can tell who owns the next decision, the process is too vague to protect the people involved.

43
00:03:07.661 --> 00:03:12.431
If every minor edit waits for a central committee, it is too heavy to help them.

44
00:03:12.431 --> 00:03:15.081
Chapter Twenty-Four names five scaling mistakes.

45
00:03:15.081 --> 00:03:18.172
A central team can become a bottleneck.

46
00:03:18.172 --> 00:03:22.146
Documentation can grow so heavy that people stop testing.

47
00:03:22.146 --> 00:03:27.004
The organization can fixate on one tool instead of shared learning.

48
00:03:27.004 --> 00:03:31.420
Teams can resist a process they did not help shape.

49
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:37.161
And leaders can expand across the whole organization before the first handoff works.

50
00:03:37.161 --> 00:03:37.391
Put those five labels on the checklist and test your own proposal against them.

51
00:03:37.415 --> 00:03:39.173
Which one is most likely here?

52
00:03:39.173 --> 00:03:47.906
In the fictional membership case, tool fixation could be tempting: the first team has a spreadsheet, so everyone is told to use the same spreadsheet.

53
00:03:47.906 --> 00:03:53.356
What matters is whether the second team can understand the hypothesis, measure, and decision.

54
00:03:53.356 --> 00:03:57.459
Another tool could carry the same record if it preserves the contract.

55
00:03:57.459 --> 00:03:59.510
Resistance can also be information.

56
00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:04.081
Ask what work the new team already does and what burden the process would add.

57
00:04:04.081 --> 00:04:09.708
A five-minute decision note may fit; a fifty-page report will likely disappear into the backlog.

58
00:04:09.708 --> 00:04:18.792
The chapter's maturity question is powerful: can a new colleague explain why the organization passed on an obvious alternative, citing the decision record?

59
00:04:18.792 --> 00:04:23.539
If the answer lives only in one person's memory, the system has not yet traveled.

60
00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:27.114
Check the next-team burden before declaring the system ready.

61
00:04:27.114 --> 00:04:31.686
Have someone outside the first team fill out the record with a fictional case.

62
00:04:31.686 --> 00:04:36.492
Note which fields they cannot interpret and which handoffs require a conversation.

63
00:04:36.492 --> 00:04:38.719
Revise the form before a live rollout.

64
00:04:38.719 --> 00:04:45.107
Keep the five book labels attached to this check; they are a useful warning, not a score or maturity ranking.

65
00:04:45.107 --> 00:04:49.386
A low score invented for the slide would add confidence without evidence.

66
00:04:49.386 --> 00:04:51.437
Chapter Twenty-Five returns to Sam.

67
00:04:51.437 --> 00:04:59.349
The book describes a calmer operating rhythm: fewer frantic searches for context, shorter decisions, and a record that people can find.

68
00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:01.224
This is the book's illustration.

69
00:05:01.224 --> 00:05:07.906
It is not evidence that our fictional membership service, AmaduTown, or this workshop has produced those outcomes.

70
00:05:07.906 --> 00:05:13.063
The story is useful because it shows what institutional memory feels like in daily work.

71
00:05:13.063 --> 00:05:15.115
Someone asks why a choice was made.

72
00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:20.155
Instead of rebuilding the argument from old messages, the team opens the decision log.

73
00:05:20.155 --> 00:05:21.972
Someone wants to repeat a test.

74
00:05:21.972 --> 00:05:27.950
Instead of copying a result without context, they can inspect the participants, measure, and revision.

75
00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.411
Notice how much of this depends on people.

76
00:05:30.411 --> 00:05:37.972
A record must be written well enough to be read; a reviewer must notice when evidence no longer fits; an owner must still decide.

77
00:05:37.972 --> 00:05:41.195
Automated routing can move a source to the right place.

78
00:05:41.195 --> 00:05:45.649
It cannot determine whether a past finding applies to a different community.

79
00:05:45.649 --> 00:05:50.748
In the storyboard, keep Sam's narrative label separate from any observed evidence card.

80
00:05:50.748 --> 00:05:55.730
We will not film a fabricated board meeting as if it happened in the present project.

81
00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:59.715
The before-and-after story can tempt us to promise relief too early.

82
00:05:59.715 --> 00:06:10.441
Ask what would have to be measured to make that claim in a real organization: time spent finding context, repeated decisions, missed handoffs, and the cost of maintaining the records.

83
00:06:10.441 --> 00:06:13.781
A calmer calendar alone would not explain why it changed.

84
00:06:13.781 --> 00:06:17.356
Keep the causal claim open until the evidence can support it.

85
00:06:17.356 --> 00:06:21.576
The lesson is a working rhythm that people can inspect, revise, and own.

86
00:06:21.576 --> 00:06:26.939
The decision-memory record is where a useful test becomes a reusable asset.

87
00:06:26.939 --> 00:06:30.514
Write what the team believed before the test.

88
00:06:30.514 --> 00:06:32.748
Link to the source evidence.

89
00:06:32.748 --> 00:06:36.323
Record what happened, including contrary observations and limits.

90
00:06:36.323 --> 00:06:43.027
State the choice, the person accountable for it, and the condition that would reopen it.

91
00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:46.664
Add the date when its context should be checked again.

92
00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:50.355
If the result contradicted the hypothesis, keep it visible.

93
00:06:50.355 --> 00:06:58.659
A later team should be able to ask a narrow question: does this past decision still apply to our people and our operating constraints?

94
00:06:58.659 --> 00:07:03.493
The book's Sam story treats searchable decisions as part of a learning system.

95
00:07:03.493 --> 00:07:07.583
We can borrow that practice without borrowing its claimed numbers.

96
00:07:07.583 --> 00:07:15.701
For the membership exercise, a record might say the short-commitment offer remains a hypothesis until an approved test is reviewed.

97
00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.808
It should not say the model works.

98
00:07:17.808 --> 00:07:25.430
Link the two-audience pitch from Lesson Fourteen, the learning plan from Lesson Eight, and the job card from Lesson Twelve.

99
00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:30.264
Now the record shows the chain from input to interpretation to test to choice.

100
00:07:30.264 --> 00:07:32.247
If a source is missing, mark it.

101
00:07:32.247 --> 00:07:36.399
Recoverability depends on honest gaps as much as on good summaries.

102
00:07:36.399 --> 00:07:38.506
Imagine the original owner leaves.

103
00:07:38.506 --> 00:07:45.695
Can the next person find the most recent source, the decision threshold, and the reason an alternative was rejected?

104
00:07:45.695 --> 00:07:47.554
If not, add that missing link.

105
00:07:47.554 --> 00:07:54.494
If an AI summary is attached, preserve the original record beside it and name the human who checked the summary.

106
00:07:54.494 --> 00:07:58.461
The memory is only as reliable as the path back to its evidence.

107
00:07:58.461 --> 00:08:02.055
A confident paragraph without source links will age badly.

108
00:08:02.055 --> 00:08:03.852
Pause and complete two pages.

109
00:08:03.852 --> 00:08:14.511
First, the scale-readiness checklist: common definition, participant boundary, measure, owner, review cadence, conflict check, privacy or billing escalation, and stop rule.

110
00:08:14.511 --> 00:08:17.795
Mark each field ready, missing, or context-dependent.

111
00:08:17.795 --> 00:08:26.843
Second, the decision-memory record: original question, evidence, test, outcome state, decision, rejected alternative, rationale, and revisit date.

112
00:08:26.843 --> 00:08:32.420
Resume when a second team could explain what it may reuse and what it must validate again.

113
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:38.369
If your current evidence is only a synthetic workshop exercise, mark the scale decision blocked.

114
00:08:38.369 --> 00:08:41.778
Do not turn a hypothetical success into a rollout plan.

115
00:08:41.778 --> 00:08:49.524
In your own organization, keep private records inside approved systems and use only public, synthetic, or approved data here.

116
00:08:49.524 --> 00:08:54.606
The book gives us Sam's operating story and a set of governance problems to watch.

117
00:08:54.606 --> 00:09:01.980
The exercise asks for a smaller, inspectable answer: can another team find the evidence and know who can stop the test?

118
00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:05.946
The final lesson returns to the decision you named at the start.

119
00:09:05.946 --> 00:09:10.470
We will choose one move for the next week and give it a real review date.

120
00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:16.791
Learning becomes infrastructure when another person can pick up the record and act with the same care.

121
00:09:16.791 --> 00:09:16.802
Learning becomes infrastructure when another person can pick up the record and act with the same care.

122
00:09:16.802 --> 00:09:22.120
End with a decision about readiness, even if the answer is wait.

123
00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:29.211
Choose one neighboring team for a future handoff only after the record survives a dry run.

124
00:09:29.211 --> 00:09:35.416
State which safeguards belong to the shared process and which must be set locally.

125
00:09:35.416 --> 00:09:37.231
This keeps a successful local experiment from becoming an organization-wide promise by accident.

126
00:09:37.271 --> 00:09:42.975
The aim is a repeatable learning practice that can carry disagreement and correction as well as success.
